{"notes":["VERDICT QUALIFICATION — 'approve' is recorded on the R13 vocabulary (approve | changes-requested) and means: the planning set is structurally sound and no change to it is being requested. It is NOT unconditional. One blocking defect was found during this review and fixed before the verdict was recorded (Finding 1); three further findings are recorded below as limitations of the eka tooling rather than of this knowledge. Approval of the artifacts themselves — moving them from draft to in-force — remains the decision-maker's, not the reviewer's.","SCOPE OF REVIEW — 17 published units of project walkie: vis:terminal-mesh-comms, fnd:tailnet-capability-baseline, fnd:terminal-audio-constraints, req:text-messaging, req:device-presence, req:voice-communication, req:file-transfer, req:offline-delivery, arc:system-overview, adr:001-transport-topology, adr:002-runtime-stack, adr:003-wire-protocol, adr:004-security-model, adr:005-no-remote-command-execution, scp:mvp-text-voicenote, plan:roadmap-v1, ses:planning-2026-08-20. Reviewed with eka validate, eka integrity check, eka get (identity, --upstream, --downstream, --timeline) and eka context --depth engineering.","FINDING 1 — BLOCKING, FIXED. Relationship targets were authored in unqualified form (e.g. 'adr:001-transport-topology'), which eka new accepts and stores verbatim, but the Runtime resolver rejects during traversal: it requires the qualified form '\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e'. Effect: eka context and eka get --upstream failed on all 16 artifacts carrying relationships — only vis:terminal-mesh-comms, which has none, was retrievable with traversal. The knowledge was published but its graph was not walkable, which would have degraded every downstream consumer including eka context and the execution flow. Root cause is not project-specific: the unqualified form is what the eka new --file batch documentation itself shows. FIX APPLIED: all 16 artifacts republished at instance version 2 with fully qualified targets. VERIFIED: all 17 units now return successfully for eka get --upstream and eka context --depth engineering; eka context on scp:mvp-text-voicenote reaches 11 units across 2 strata via transitive closure, with 7 upstream, 7 dependencies, 11 constraints and 4 decisions resolved.","FINDING 2 — NOT FIXABLE IN THIS PROJECT, RECORDED AS A LIMITATION. eka get --downstream returns no downstream array at all, even for correctly qualified edges. Confirmed as a CLI-level defect rather than a data problem by a control test against the pre-existing nest-desacorp-web project, where a properly qualified 'discusses' edge also produced no reverse edge. Impact on walkie: none for R10 traceability or for eka context, both of which traverse upstream. Impact in general: reverse-edge discovery ('what references this?') is unavailable, so the blast radius of changing an artifact cannot be read from the tool.","FINDING 3 — SELF-DISCLOSED SIDE EFFECT, RESOLVED ON THE LIVE LINE. While probing whether eka relate could correct Finding 1 without instance churn, a relate was run against req:file-transfer. eka relate adds edges and never normalises them, so instance 1 of that line briefly carried BOTH the unqualified and the qualified derives-from edge. Instance 2, published from the corrected batch, carries only the qualified edge, so the current line is clean; instance 1 retains the duplicate in history, which is correct under immutability rather than something to be erased. Recorded here so the timeline is explainable to a later reader.","FINDING 4 — OUT OF SCOPE, ACTIONABLE ELSEWHERE. The same unqualified-reference defect affects the operator's other projects: 199 unqualified relationship targets in nest-desacorp-web (against 435 qualified) and at least one in the eka project itself, where eka get eka/adr:001-identity-serialization --upstream fails on the target 'req:prd'. eka context is therefore broken for affected subjects in those projects. No action taken here; raised for the operator's attention.","STRUCTURE — PASS. Derived classification is correct across the set: 8 units in Discovery at stratum 1 (vis/intent, 2x fnd/research, 5x req/requirements), 6 in Architecture at stratum 2 (arc/architecture, 5x adr/decisions), 2 in Planning at stratum 3 (scp and plan, both planning), 1 in Execution at stratum 4 (ses, carrying no dimension, correctly). eka validate on the repository returns PASS with 0 errors and 0 warnings. eka integrity check returns 0 violations across 1325 payloads and 905 references after the republish.","STATE HONESTY — PASS. Every knowledge artifact sits in its scaffolded initial state: contentState draft for the living variant, proposed for the ADR variant, planningState draft for the plan. No state was advanced during authoring, so nothing in this set claims to be in force. eka context on scp:mvp-text-voicenote confirms this: all 11 constraint units report state draft or proposed, so no higher-authority knowledge is being contradicted, because none is yet in force. Change-log entries match each state vector exactly, and author identity and dates were taken by the CLI from git configuration rather than hand-written.","RELATIONSHIP RESOLVABILITY — PASS, GATED. eka publish --all refuses the whole batch before writing anything if any draft references a target that is neither a pending draft nor an already-stored object. Both publish runs passed that gate, so every edge in the set resolves to a real unit. Topological ordering was applied automatically, so no artifact was published before something it references.","CONTENT JUDGEMENT — the set is internally consistent and does not overclaim. Three specific points were checked because they are the places where planning knowledge usually lies. (a) The sub-100 ms latency figure is stated as calculated rather than measured, is scoped to direct paths only, and is deliberately excluded from the MVP scope — it is not presented as satisfied. (b) fnd:terminal-audio-constraints states plainly that no ARM device has been profiled, and the roadmap makes the audio spike a blocking phase 0 with its failure consequence defined in advance, so the riskiest assumption is not load-bearing on an unverified claim. (c) adr:004-security-model enumerates its own limitations — unencrypted local history, no key rotation, no multi-device identity, dependence on a correctly configured tailnet ACL — rather than presenting the model as complete.","ONE SUBSTANTIVE DISAGREEMENT, RECORDED NOT SUPPRESSED. The reviewing agent recommended a coordinator-only topology with a pure peer-to-peer data plane; the decision-maker chose hybrid with relay fallback. The disagreement is preserved in ses:planning-2026-08-20 and in the consequences section of adr:001-transport-topology, together with the mitigation it implies: the direct-versus-relay ratio must be a first-class metric, because a relay fallback that silently absorbs a tailnet which never achieves direct connectivity would otherwise make the decision unobservable in production.","WHAT BLOCKS APPROVAL — nothing structural. Finding 1 is fixed and verified. Findings 2 and 4 are defects in the eka tooling itself, not in this knowledge, and should be reported through eka feedback. Finding 3 is disclosed and resolved on the live line. Approval now depends only on the decision-maker confirming that these artifacts should move from draft to in-force, which is a judgement about intent and not about structure."],"role":"review","verdict":"approve"}